# Ooda CLI — argv Parsing

File: ooda/docs/argv.oot

The `ooda` binary parses argv in `ooda/main.oo` and dispatches to one of 17 subcommands. The parsing is intentionally minimal: `main.oo` reads `argv[0]` as the subcommand, slices `argv[1..]` as the per-command tail, and hands that tail to the per-command parser.

## Two-tier parse

The parse happens in two tiers. Tier 1 is in `main.oo` and decides the subcommand. Tier 2 is in the per-command file (e.g. `cli/build/host_build.oo`, `cli/fix/fix.oo`) and decides the subcommand's own flags.

| Tier | Where | Parses | Sees |
|------|-------|--------|------|
| 1 | `ooda/main.oo` | `argv[0]` only | subcommand name; errors on missing or unknown |
| 2 | per-command | the rest of argv | the per-command flag set |

There is no global flag layer beyond help and version. `ooda --help` prints the help above and exits 0, while bare `ooda` opens ooda-tui. `ooda --version` prints the version string (same as `ooda version`). Beyond those two, flags belong to each subcommand: a global flag layer would have to be threaded through every per-command parser, and the 8 caps already cover what flags would do.

## Per-command flag set

Each subcommand owns its own argv parse loop. The loop walks `args`, checks `str_slice(a, 0, 1) == "-"`, and dispatches by exact match or `=`-form prefix. Below is the full per-command flag table, derived from the source.

### build

| Flag | Type | Effect |
|------|------|--------|
| `--target {native,llvm,aarch64,wasm,...}` | str | set the backend |
| `-o <path>` | path | output binary path |
| `-O0`-`-O3`, `-Oz` | str | opt level (passed to oodac) |
| `-Opgo` | str | PGO mode |
| `--lto` | bool | enable LTO |
| `--release` | bool | release build |
| `--pgo-generate` / `-fprofile-generate` | bool | emit PGO profile |
| `--pgo-use=<p>` / `-fprofile-use=<p>` | path | reuse PGO profile |
| `-fsanitize=<a>` | list | add sanitizer |
| `--sanitize=<a>` | list | add sanitizer (with prefix) |
| `--fuzz` | bool | fuzzer sanitizer |

### run

| Flag | Type | Effect |
|------|------|--------|
| `--engine {native,bc,...}` | str | engine selector (residual `--json-errors` is refused) |

### test

| Flag | Type | Effect |
|------|------|--------|
| `--engine=bc` / `-bc` | str | use the BC engine |
| `--json` / `-json` | bool | emit JSON instead of human output |

### bench

Moved verb. Exit 2. Use `bb bench`.

### init

Takes one positional arg (project name). Defaults to `myproject` when missing. No flags.

### context

| Flag | Type | Effect |
|------|------|--------|
| `--llm` | bool | stream the codex file instead of listing .oo files |
| `--json` | bool | emit the listing as JSON |

### health

Moved verb. Exit 2. Use `bb health`.

### fix

| Flag | Type | Effect |
|------|------|--------|
| `--yes` | bool | skip the TTY confirmation prompt |
| `--dry-run` | bool | print proposed edits without writing |

### digest

Moved verb. Exit 2. Use `bb digest`.

### fmt

| Flag | Type | Effect |
|------|------|--------|
| `--check` | bool | report but do not rewrite |
| `--all` | bool | walk the dir; default is one file |

### token (and 12 sub-subcommands)

The `ooda token` subcommand takes one of 12 sub-subcommand names plus a global format flag.

| Flag | Type | Effect |
|------|------|--------|
| `--human` / `-h` | bool | switch output from JSON (default) to human |
| `--format=human` | str | same as `--human` |
| `--format=json` | str | explicit JSON (the default) |

The token rest list starts at the sub-subcommand name (`ooda token status` → rest `["status"]`). Subcommands: `status`, `report`, `audit`, `query`, `ask`, `schema`, `diff`, `tail`, `fix`, `snapshot`, `enforce`, `ingest`, `heatmap`. Each is forwarded by `cli/token.oo`.

### install

| Flag | Type | Effect |
|------|------|--------|
| `--from <path>` | path | source pack dir; falls back to `OODA_CHECKOUT` or `.` |
| `--to <path>` | path | install root; falls back to `OODA_FS_WRITEDIR` |

Both must validate as safe paths (no traversal, no `..`, no `;`). `--to` must be under `OODA_FS_WRITEDIR`.

### update

| Flag | Type | Effect |
|------|------|--------|
| `--yes` | bool | pass `--yes` to install.sh (no prompts) |
| `--dry-run` | bool | pass `--dry-run` to install.sh (preview only) |
| `--no-modify-shell` | bool | pass `--no-modify-shell` to install.sh |
| `--installer <path>` / `--installer=<path>` | path | local install.sh instead of the canonical URL |

No positionals. Unknown flags, bare `--installer` with no path, and unsafe installer paths (empty, `..`, `~`, quotes, `$`, backtick, `;`, `|`, `&`) fail closed with exit 2.

### opm, lsp, mcp (spawned)

The whole rest of argv is passed verbatim to the spawned binary. No flag layer on the ooda side. The spawned binary does its own parse.

## How to add a new flag

The convention is local. Pick the file that owns the subcommand (e.g. `cli/build/host_build.oo` for build flags) and add a branch to its argv loop. Use `=`-form for values that look like paths, plain `-flag` for booleans, and reject unknown flags loudly with `process_exit(2)`. Never silently ignore a flag.

## How to add a new subcommand

Do not add in-process handlers except help/version. Language verbs get a `forward_to_cli` branch in `ooda/main.oo` and a `router_help()` listing. External drivers call `spawn`. Moved verbs print a moved line and `process_exit(2)`.
